Overview
Kubernetes applications use YAML and JSON manifest files to define infrastructure, Pods, Namespaces, and resource
configurations. As repositories grow, manually reviewing multiple files to find resource settings can become
time-consuming.
This project provides a local CLI tool that reads and analyzes Kubernetes manifest files. It extracts relevant resource
information and presents it in a clear terminal table, making configuration review easier.
Problem Statement
Developers often need to inspect several Kubernetes manifest files to find:
• Pod and Namespace information.
• Container names.
• CPU requests and limits.
• Memory requests and limits.
• Resource values that are missing.
KUBERNETES CLI CONFIGURATION AND VISIBILITY 2
• Resource values explicitly set to zero.
Manual inspection can be repetitive and error-prone. This tool automates the inspection process and makes the difference
between an unconfigured value and an explicit zero value 
Features
• Read local Kubernetes YAML and JSON manifest files.
• Process multiple files and multi-document YAML streams.
• Identify supported Kubernetes resource kinds: Pod and Namespace.
• Extract resource names, namespaces, labels, and container names.
• Extract CPU and memory requests and limits.
• Preserve the original Kubernetes quantity strings, such as 500m,
1Gi, and 256Mi.
• Distinguish missing or null values from explicit zero values.
• Display extracted information in an aligned terminal table.
• Sort output by namespace and resource name.
• Report file access and parsing errors.
• Continue processing other documents where possible when an
individual document is invalid.
Scope and Limitations
Supported in the initial version
• Local YAML and JSON files.
• Multi-document YAML streams separated by ---.
• Pod and Namespace resource kinds.
• Container CPU and memory requests and limits.
• Offline manifest inspection.
Not included in the initial version
• Connecting to a live Kubernetes cluster.
• Reading Kubernetes cluster credentials or kubeconfig contexts.
• Monitoring the real-time status of running Pods.
• Modifying manifest files.
• Deploying or managing Kubernetes resources.
This project inspects local configuration files only. It does not determine the operational status of a live
Kubernetes cluster.
