How It Works
The tool follows this processing flow:
Input: The user provides one or more local YAML or JSON manifest
file paths through the CLI.
Validation: The tool checks whether the files exist and can be
accessed.
Parsing: YAML is processed with yaml.safe_load_all() and JSON
with Python's standard JSON parser.
Document validation: Kubernetes object fields such as
apiVersion, kind, and metadata are checked.
Resource identification: Supported resource kinds, Pod and
Namespace, are identified. Unsupported kinds are skipped and counted.
Data extraction: Metadata, container names, CPU requests and
limits, and memory requests and limits are extracted.
Normalization: Extracted values are organized into structured
records. Missing, null, zero, and configured values are distinguished.
Output: Records are sorted and displayed in a readable terminal
table, with diagnostics and a summary.
Processing Architecture
Kubernetes YAML / JSON Manifests
 |
 v
 CLI Argument Parser
 |
 v
 File Validation
 |
 v
 Safe File Parser
 |
 v
 Kubernetes Resource Identification
 |
 v
 Metadata and Resource Extraction
 |
 v
 Data Normalization
 |
 v
 Terminal Table Renderer
 |
 v
 Results / Diagnostics / Exit Status
KUBERNETES CLI CONFIGURATION AND VISIBILITY 4
Resource Value Display
One of the main requirements is to distinguish between a resource value that is not configured and a resource value
explicitly set to zero.
----------------------------------------------------------------------- State Meaning Display ----------------------- -----------------------
----------------------- Configured A valid resource Original value, quantity is declared e.g. 500m
Explicit zero The resource key exists 0 and is set to zero
Missing or null The resource key is [NONE] absent or its value is null -----------------------------------------------------------------------
This avoids treating a missing configuration as if it were an explicitly configured zero.
Installation
Prerequisites
• Python 3.10 or later is recommended.
• pip for installing Python dependencies.
• Git to clone the repository.
Clone the repository
git clone <your-repository-url>
cd <your-repository-folder>
Replace the placeholders with your actual GitHub repository URL and folder name.
Create and activate a virtual environment
macOS / Linux
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
Windows
py -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\activate
Install dependencies
python -m pip install PyYAML
Testing dependencies can be installed with:
python -m pip install pytest
The exact installation and run commands may be updated as the project
implementation is completed.
Usage
The planned CLI accepts one or more manifest file paths. The following examples illustrate the intended command format;
use the command name and options implemented in the repository.
python <cli-entrypoint>.py path/to/manifest.yaml
Pass multiple files:
python <cli-entrypoint>.py path/to/pods.yaml path/to/namespaces.json
Display help:
python <cli-entrypoint>.py --help
The CLI entry-point filename and exact command syntax should be updated here once they are finalized in the
implementation.
KUBERNETES CLI CONFIGURATION AND VISIBILITY 5
Example Output
The terminal report is intended to show the resource, namespace, container, and CPU/memory request and limit
information.
Illustrative layout:
+-----------+-----------+-----------+-------------+----------+---------------+------------+
| Namespace | Pod | Container | CPU Request | CPU Limit| Memory Request| Memory Limit|
+-----------+-----------+-----------+-------------+----------+---------------+------------+
| default | web-pod | web | 250m | 500m | 128Mi | 256Mi |
| default | worker | worker | [NONE] | 0 | 256Mi | [NONE] |
+-----------+-----------+-----------+-------------+----------+---------------+------------+
This is an illustrative example of the planned output format, not a captured result from a completed implementation.
Testing
The project plans to include the following tests.
Unit testing
• Valid YAML and JSON files.
• Deeply nested configuration structures.
• Multi-document YAML files.
• Missing, null, and zero resource values.
• Invalid file paths and permission errors.
• Malformed configuration files.
Integration testing
• Complete CLI workflow.
• Extraction from representative Kubernetes manifests.
• Terminal table formatting.
• Output and error handling.
• CLI exit-code behavior.
End-to-end testing
Run the tool against a test directory containing 50 or more mixed YAML and JSON manifest files. Verify resource totals,
skipped resource counts, parsing results, and terminal output.
Project Goals
The target goals defined for the project are:
Metric Target ------------------------------ ---------------------------------------------- Must-have feature completion 100% Automated
test coverage At least 85% Processing time Under 2 seconds for 1,000 declared resources Memory consumption Target
below 50 MB RSS Error handling Graceful handling of malformed input files Missing vs. zero distinction No ambiguous
states
These are project targets to be validated through testing and benchmarking.
Future Enhancements
After the core requirements are completed, possible enhancements include:
• Machine-readable JSON output: Add a --json option for
downstream tools and CI pipelines.
• Recursive directory scanning: Scan directories and their
KUBERNETES CLI CONFIGURATION AND VISIBILITY 6
subdirectories for manifest files.
• Standard input support: Accept manifests through stdin.
• More workload controllers: Inspect Pod templates in Deployments,
StatefulSets, and DaemonSets.
• Resource quantity conversion: Convert CPU quantities to cores
and memory quantities to MiB/GiB for easier comparison.
• Cross-platform CI: Run automated tests on Ubuntu, macOS, and
Windows.
